  :adr/body "# ADR 2799008155: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 8155 Fur and Leather Preparing Machine Operators plant scheduling/logistics coordination coverage

## Status

Accepted

## Context

This is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the
`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing
ADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an
autonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a
`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a
robotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/
administrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that
gates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates
high-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 8155
-- Fur and Leather Preparing Machine Operators** only. This is Wave3
(production/trades); Wave0/1/2 are 100% complete, and this actor
continues the 81xx plant-and-machine-operator range
(`kotoba.occupation.wave/wave-of \"8155\"` resolves to wave 3).

`gh api repos/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8155` returned a clean
404 before any work began -- this was a genuinely fresh scaffold, not a
resume of prior partial work.

The `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"8155\" ...}` entry
was verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M
-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-
occupation \\\"8155\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/
technology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative
path), and its `:name` read exactly \"Fur and Leather Preparing Machine
Operators\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR
the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies
[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`.
This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the `8155` entry to
`:implemented`.

**Chemical tanning-machine hazard dimension.** Fur and Leather
Preparing Machine Operators run tanning/dressing machinery (fleshing,
tanning-drum, dyeing, splitting and staking machines) using chemical
processing agents -- a real chemical-exposure hazard, alongside
heavy-machinery hazard (crush/entanglement injury from rollers and
drums). This actor does NOT model direct processing-machine operation
authority or chemical-safety-clearance authority. It is deliberately
restricted to PLANT SCHEDULING and LOGISTICS COORDINATION around the
occupation (production-run/inventory/progress-record logging,
crew/shift scheduling, safety-concern escalation, and
processing-chemicals/raw-hide procurement coordination) -- never the
processing-operation-execution decision or the chemical-safety-
clearance decision itself, and never an override of a plant safety
officer's judgment. The closed proposal-op allowlist structurally
excludes any op that could directly finalize such a decision, and the
governor independently, permanently hard-blocks any proposal whose
rationale text names such a finalization action, regardless of the
declared `:op` or confidence -- this is not an escalation path a human
can approve through, it is an outright, structural refusal at every
maturity level.

The reference implementation mirrored closely for module SHAPE is
[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8122`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8122)
(Metal Finishing, Plating and Coating Machine Operators -- a closely
related chemical-processing industrial-machine safety pattern),
independently re-cloned and read in full this session, and
independently re-verified as green (fresh clone, `clojure -M:test`)
before being used as a template. `cloud-itonami-isco-7535` (Pelt
Dressers, Tanners and Fellmongers -- the craft-occupation sibling of
this same fur/leather domain, `tannerycoord.*`) was also cloned and
read for domain vocabulary, since it is the closer domain match, but
8122's module SHAPE was mirrored per the task's explicit reference
pointer. Both references' `{store,advisor,governor,actor}` module
split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph shape, closed op-allowlist and
scope-exclusion self-trip-guard design translate almost 1:1 onto this
occupation's own chemical-hazard-adjacent coordination scope, so this
ADR's `furleathercoord.*` modules follow the same shape with domain
terms substituted (plater -> operator, plating facility/plant -> fur
and leather preparing plant, plating-operation-execution ->
processing-operation-execution).

## Decision

Publish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8155` as an OSS
documentation/logistics-coordination actor for fur and leather
preparing machine operators, following the SAME governed-actor
architecture as every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this
fleet: **Fur and Leather Preparing Plant Scheduling Coordination
Advisor ⊣ FurLeatherCoordGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph
(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the
reference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the
`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),
with an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via
`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.

This actor is deliberately **plant scheduling/logistics coordination
ONLY, never processing-machine operation authority** -- it must NEVER
be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose exercising a
processing-operation-execution decision or a chemical-safety-clearance
decision.

### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)

- `:log-work-record` -- production-run/inventory/progress data
  logging only
- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/shift/task scheduling proposal
  only
- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a chemical-exposure/
  heavy-machinery(crush/entanglement from rollers and drums)/
  equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates immediately, NEVER
  auto-commit-eligible
- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- processing-chemicals/raw-hide
  procurement proposal only

No op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase,
resembles a processing-operation-execution finalization decision or a
chemical-safety-clearance finalization decision -- these are
structurally absent from the op-allowlist entirely, not merely gated
behind escalation.

### Five HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)

1. **Operator unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an
   independently registered operator (crew member) record
   (`:no-operator`). Never trusts the proposal's own claim of who the
   operator is.
2. **Plant unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an
   independently registered fur/leather preparing plant/line record
   (`:no-plant`).
3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD
   block (`:no-actuation`; the governor never dispatches hardware
   itself and never operates fur/leather preparing machinery itself).
4. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op
   allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`).
5. **Scope-excluded action** -- the proposal's `:op` or `:rationale`
   must never name a processing-operation-execution finalization, a
   chemical-safety-clearance finalization, or a plant-safety-officer-
   judgment override (`:scope-excluded-action`), REGARDLESS of the
   declared `:op` or confidence. This is a hard, PERMANENT block --
   there is no maturity level or confidence at which this actor may
   auto-commit or human-approve its way past this rule; it is an
   outright refusal, not an escalation.

### Escalation gates (always human sign-off)

- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of
  confidence (governor's always-escalate path). The only channel by
  which the robot may surface a chemical-exposure/machinery-crush-
  entanglement/equipment-condition observation -- it never resolves
  the concern itself.
- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds
  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000) -- always needs human sign-off,
  independent of confidence (boundary verified as exclusive by
  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`: exactly-at-threshold
  commits, over-threshold escalates).
- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for
  any op.)

### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, CLAUDE.md)

A governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as bare NOUNS (e.g.
\"pelt\", \"fur\", \"chemical\") can accidentally match inside the mock
advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,
self-blocking the actor's own happy path -- and this vertical's domain
vocabulary is an especially sharp case of that risk, because routine
work-log/task text legitimately names tanning/dressing machinery and
its chemistry by name (e.g. a task literally described as \"routine fur
and leather preparing task with tanning drum rollers and chemical
processing agents\" -- equipment/chemical identification, not a
proposal to finalize a processing operation or clear the plant as
safe). `furleathercoord.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased
exclusively as finalization ACTION PHRASES (\"finalize the tanning
operation\", \"declare the plant safety cleared\", \"override the plant
safety officer's judgment\" -- the exact three phrases specified by the
task, plus their closely-related variants), never bare nouns, and is
checked only against the proposal's `:rationale` field (the advisor's
own generated text), never against raw `:task` input text.
`furleathercoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-
self-trip-on-scope-exclusion` is a dedicated regression test driving
the default mock-advisor across all four allowed ops with a task field
containing exactly those bare domain nouns, asserting none of them
ever hard-trip the finalization-scope rule. Green on first run.

