## Alternatives considered

- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-processing-run`/
  `:approve-chemical-safety-clearance` that is merely gated behind an
  always-escalate path: rejected outright per the task's explicit
  guidance -- an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to
  finalize a processing operation or a chemical-safety clearance
  exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human gate. For
  a decision whose failure mode is chemical exposure or
  crush/entanglement injury, the capability itself must not exist in
  this actor at all.
- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"pelt\" \"fur\"
  \"chemical\"]`): rejected outright per the task's explicit
  instruction and the fleet-known self-trip bug class described above
  -- this domain's own vocabulary necessarily discusses tanning/
  dressing machinery and processing agents by name to do its
  logging/scheduling job, so a bare-noun list would self-block the
  actor's own legitimate proposals whenever a task description
  mentions pelt, fur or chemical agents.
- Naming the crew-member field `:plater-id` (mirroring
  `cloud-itonami-isco-8122`'s own field name literally, which itself
  deliberately avoided \"operator\" to sidestep a naming collision with
  that repo's deploying-business sense of \"operator\"): rejected --
  the task's own domain design explicitly specifies \"operator/plant
  record\" (matching the ISCO title \"...Machine Operators\" literally
  and mirroring the more recent `cloud-itonami-isco-8156` sibling's
  same resolution), and this repo's own docs do not create the same
  collision, so `:operator-id`/`:plant-id` is used instead of a
  borrowed domain-specific term.

